`]}),e.jsx(u,{type:"info",title:"Bonus chapter",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Bonus 1 is required reading"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"What segmentation changes, with a spelled-out number",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A tokenizer learns its pieces from data. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Byte-pair encoding (BPE)"})," builds its vocabulary by merging units that often occur next to each other in the training text, so frequent sequences become single tokens ",e.jsx(n,{id:"sennrich2016",children:"Sennrich et al., 2016"}),". Byte-level BPE starts from units derived from the bytes of the text and applies its learned merges on top of them ",e.jsx(n,{id:"gpt2-2019",children:"Radford et al., 2019"}),". The course tokenizer is of this kind. Because the merges follow frequency, the pieces need not align with words."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"How a tokenizer segments text changes three things downstream. A text that splits into more tokens fills more sequence positions, so attention has more work to do. A bigger vocabulary means a bigger input embedding table and a bigger output layer, since each holds one row per vocabulary entry. And segmentation can expose or obscure regular structure, across languages and in numbers."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Spelling out the number from the earlier trace shows the first effect. With the same course tokenizer ",e.jsx(n,{id:"pleias2025monad",children:"PleIAs, 2025"}),", again without added special tokens, the numeral sentence has 7 tokens and the spelled-out sentence 14:"]}),e.jsx(t.figure,{"data-rehype-pretty-code-figure":"",children:e.jsx(t.pre,{tabIndex:"0","data-language":"plaintext","data-theme":"github-dark-default github-light-default",children:e.jsxs(t.code,{"data-language":"plaintext","data-theme":"github-dark-default github-light-default",style:{display:"grid"},children:[e.jsx(t.span,{"data-line":"",children:e.jsx(t.span,{children:'"The price is three hundred forty-two euros."'})}),`
